CALL newPutForth(413, 1, 'Schools couldn\'t get the ventilation upgrades, the substitute teachers, or the rapid tests they needed. The pandemic exposed how underfunded our public schools are. That\'s not a political statement — it\'s a resource allocation failure that put kids and teachers at risk.');
CALL newPutForth(431, 1, 'Bikes contribute nothing measurable to road wear. The pavement damage formula is brutal to heavy vehicles and laughably forgiving to bikes. You\'d need hundreds of thousands of bike trips to match a single heavy truck. Taxing cyclists for road upkeep is a non-starter.');
CALL newPutForth(432, 1, 'If we want better roads, we want more bikes. Subsidize bike infrastructure, make streets safer for cyclists, waive bike registration fees — whatever it takes to get more people out of cars and onto bikes. The road math works heavily in bikes\' favor.');
CALL newPutForth(436, 1, 'Road pavement damage isn\'t linear — it scales with the fourth power of axle weight. Doubling a vehicle\'s weight doesn\'t double road damage; it multiplies it by sixteen. That\'s why bikes are essentially free riders and heavy trucks are enormously destructive.');
CALL newPutForth(437, 1, 'One Hummer does the same road damage as 364,520 bike trips. That\'s not an argument against Hummers per se — it\'s an argument for not taxing bikes to fund road repairs they didn\'t cause.');
CALL newPutForth(438, 1, 'The fourth-power law for pavement damage is well-established engineering. Axle weight raised to the fourth power determines how much a vehicle destroys the road beneath it. Bikes are functionally weightless in that calculation.');
CALL newPutForth(453, 1, 'Extending road life is expensive. The cheapest intervention is to shift some trips from cars to bikes. Bikes cause negligible road wear. Every car trip replaced by a bike trip is money saved on road maintenance.');
CALL newPutForth(471, 1, 'Some roads would genuinely serve people better as bike paths, pedestrian zones, or public squares. The car isn\'t sacred. Reclaiming road space where the math supports it is good urban planning, not an attack on drivers.');
CALL newPutForth(472, 1, 'Pike Place Market would be better without through-traffic. Close the main road to private vehicles, keep delivery access, and you\'ve improved the experience for the thousands of pedestrians who actually go there.');
CALL newPutForth(473, 1, 'The road through Pike Place Market is a bottleneck that serves almost no one well. Cars crawl through at pedestrian pace. It\'s not a real throughput route — it\'s a lane that consumes public space for minimal transport benefit.');
CALL newPutForth(520, 1, 'Trained reporters with editors and institutional accountability generally beat unfiltered amateur content for accuracy and depth. Professional media has serious problems. It also has serious value that\'s easy to take for granted.');
CALL newPutForth(572, 1, 'Government execution matters as much as government intent. Deciding to build a lid over I-5 is the easy part. Managing the design, funding, contracting, and construction to actually deliver it — that\'s where public projects succeed or fail.');
CALL newPutForth(573, 1, 'Lidding I-5 through downtown Seattle would reconnect neighborhoods severed by the highway, reduce noise and air pollution, and create acres of usable public space. It\'s ambitious and expensive. It\'s also worth doing.');
CALL newPutForth(682, 1, 'Homelessness is a crisis — not just an inconvenience or a policy disagreement. People are dying on Seattle\'s streets. If you wouldn\'t accept this level of crisis in any other domain of public life, you shouldn\'t accept it here.');
CALL newPutForth(683, 1, 'Making life harder for people already living outside is not a policy. It\'s cruelty. If someone is sleeping on the street, the goal should be getting them into shelter — not making the street more punishing to prove a political point.');
CALL newPutForth(685, 1, 'Encampments exist until there\'s enough shelter. Until then, basic sanitation — portable toilets, trash collection — is harm reduction. You can\'t address a crisis by making it dirtier.');
CALL newPutForth(690, 1, 'Sanitation at encampments is better than nothing, but it doesn\'t solve the problem. Without shelter beds, service coordination, and mental health support, you\'re managing a crisis you should be ending.');
CALL newPutForth(691, 1, 'Unsanctioned encampments tend to expand when left unmanaged. That\'s an argument for moving faster on the shelter side so there\'s an actual alternative, not for tolerating indefinite growth of improvised camps.');
CALL newPutForth(692, 1, 'First priority: get people out of the elements. Before debating navigation centers versus permanent supportive housing, acknowledge that no one should be sleeping outside in this climate. Shelter first, then everything else.');
CALL newPutForth(693, 1, 'Sanctioned camps with services, or designated shelter villages with centralized support — either beats improvised camps scattered across the city with no coordination. You can address homelessness more humanely with a plan.');
CALL newPutForth(694, 1, 'King County has struggled to convert emergency shelter into permanent supportive housing at anything like the scale the crisis demands. Political will and adequate resourcing have both been intermittent at best.');
CALL newPutForth(703, 1, 'Vacant city lots sitting unused while people sleep outside is a policy failure. Micro-shelter villages on those lots aren\'t a permanent solution, but they\'re a faster path to dignity than waiting years for permanent housing.');
CALL newPutForth(710, 1, 'This isn\'t something Seattle can solve by ignoring it or tolerating it indefinitely. Homelessness at this scale requires deliberate intervention from city and county government — with resources, accountability, and clear metrics.');
CALL newPutForth(712, 1, 'I\'ll support new homelessness spending when I\'m convinced it\'s effective. Real accountability, not just intent. Before asking for more tax revenue, show me the money already allocated is being well managed.');
CALL newPutForth(715, 1, 'Most of Seattle\'s homeless population has local roots, despite the narrative that people choose Seattle for its services. Treating the crisis as a magnet problem misidentifies both the cause and the solution.');
CALL newPutForth(716, 1, 'Many of Seattle\'s unhoused have family here — kids, partners, parents. The idea that they should just move somewhere cheaper ignores the human ties that bind people to a place. That\'s not a policy; it\'s indifference.');
CALL newPutForth(717, 1, 'A lot of people experiencing homelessness in Seattle are still working — in construction, in service jobs, in the gig economy. They can\'t afford housing on what they earn. They\'re not transient; they\'re local workers without a home.');
CALL newPutForth(718, 1, 'If reallocation has genuinely been tried and exhausted and the crisis persists at this scale, then yes — additional revenue is warranted. Homelessness costs more untreated than treated. The fiscal math eventually supports intervention.');
CALL newPutForth(719, 1, 'Seattle hasn\'t run out of space. The constraint is zoning, not land. Single-family zones across most of the city represent enormous potential for gentle density — duplexes, triplexes, accessory dwelling units.');
CALL newPutForth(720, 1, 'Seventy percent of Seattle\'s residential land is zoned exclusively for single-family homes. That\'s the root of the housing shortage. You cannot build out of a housing crisis when most of the city is legally off-limits to anything denser.');
CALL newPutForth(721, 1, 'A city that prices out working-class and middle-class people stops being a city in any meaningful sense. The whole point of urban density is that people of different income levels can share the same space and economy.');
CALL newPutForth(722, 1, 'Duplexes, triplexes, townhouses, and mid-rise apartments should be the baseline across Seattle, not the exception requiring a variance. Upzoning as the default, and let the market build what people need.');
CALL newPutForth(726, 1, 'You need an address to apply for most jobs. You need a phone number for callbacks. These aren\'t minor inconveniences — they\'re structural barriers that make escaping homelessness significantly harder.');
CALL newPutForth(733, 1, 'Roads degrade from environmental factors regardless of traffic — freeze-thaw cycles, sun, moisture. Heavy vehicles still accelerate degradation dramatically beyond that baseline. Bikes barely register.');
CALL newPutForth(736, 1, 'More housing supply puts downward pressure on prices. That\'s not controversial economics — it\'s supply and demand. Seattle rents keep climbing because demand grows faster than supply. The solution is to build more.');
CALL newPutForth(737, 1, 'Developers build luxury first because the margin is better. That\'s rational in a constrained market. Loosen zoning so they have to compete, and the whole market eventually moves downmarket.');
CALL newPutForth(739, 1, 'More units of any type — luxury or workforce — add to the overall housing stock and soften prices region-wide. It works slowly and imperfectly, but that\'s how supply-side dynamics function.');
CALL newPutForth(740, 1, 'Luxury buildings draw high earners out of older stock, freeing those units for middle-income renters. Filtering works — not perfectly, not immediately, but directionally right. New supply at any level helps.');
CALL newPutForth(741, 1, 'Gentrification replaces affordable units with premium ones. Even when total unit count stays flat, the mix shifts against lower-income residents. Volume matters, but so does the affordability distribution of what gets built.');
CALL newPutForth(836, 1, 'Home prices in Seattle are unacceptably high for working people. A city where only the wealthy can afford to stay is a worse city economically and socially. This is a problem that demands a policy response.');
CALL newPutForth(1012, 1, 'Seattle keeps attracting people — tech industry, outdoor culture, food scene. It\'s a genuinely desirable place. That desirability is what makes the housing problem so stubborn and so urgent.');
CALL newPutForth(1141, 1, 'Concentrating population in cities is more efficient per capita in almost every measurable way — energy use, land consumption, transit infrastructure, emissions. Smart growth policy should make cities more appealing to live in.');
CALL newPutForth(1142, 1, 'If you want to live in the city, you should be able to live in the city. That means enough housing supply at enough price points that the city isn\'t reserved for those who already got there when prices were lower.');
CALL newPutForth(1143, 1, 'Affordable housing isn\'t a charity program — it\'s infrastructure. A city that prices out nurses and teachers and construction workers has a serious functional problem, not just a social one.');
CALL newPutForth(1144, 1, 'Every unit built in a dense urban area is several acres of suburban sprawl that didn\'t have to happen. Cities absorbing growth protects forests, farmland, and the rural character people in those areas want to preserve.');
CALL newPutForth(1145, 1, 'If you live in a city, change is the deal. Streets change, buildings change, neighborhoods change. Trying to freeze your neighborhood at the moment you arrived isn\'t stewardship — it\'s NIMBYism dressed up as love of place.');
CALL newPutForth(1251, 1, 'The denser a city, the more efficiently it functions. More people sharing infrastructure, transit, and public space — that\'s how cities create value. Sprawl undermines the fundamental logic of urban living.');
CALL newPutForth(1252, 1, 'Single-family-only zoning prevents duplexes, triplexes, and apartment buildings. It\'s the primary legal instrument of the housing shortage. You can\'t solve scarcity while keeping most land legally off-limits to additional households.');
CALL newPutForth(1253, 1, 'Most of Seattle should be upzoned — not to highrise density everywhere, but to gentle density. Duplexes, accessory dwelling units, small apartment buildings. That\'s a reasonable change that would make a large difference.');
CALL newPutForth(1254, 1, 'Parks, greenways, open plazas — they\'re not luxuries. Density works better when people have places to decompress. Open space should be part of the urban plan, not squeezed out by development pressure.');
CALL newPutForth(1255, 1, 'A neighborhood zoned universally for triplexes isn\'t radical. It barely changes the streetscape. And it allows three households where only one was legally permitted before — meaningful progress with minimal disruption.');
CALL newPutForth(1256, 1, 'Dense doesn\'t mean infinitely dense. There\'s a point where adding more people to a fixed space creates costs faster than benefits. Good planning finds the optimum — it doesn\'t just chase maximum density.');
CALL newPutForth(1257, 1, 'Dense cities can have better parks, more walkable streets, better transit, better local businesses, and better social fabric than sprawling counterparts. Density done well is enjoyable. It requires intentional design.');
CALL newPutForth(1259, 1, 'Rural upbringings can be beautiful, but they can also shelter kids from complexity — different people, different ideas, different ways of life. Insulation from the world\'s diversity leaves kids less prepared for it.');
CALL newPutForth(1260, 1, 'Hong Kong and Taipei demonstrate that high-density urban environments can be highly livable — excellent transit, good public spaces, functioning economies. Density isn\'t the enemy of quality of life.');
CALL newPutForth(1262, 1, 'A city with uniform density from edge to center is badly designed. The goal should be a gradient — denser near transit corridors and commercial centers, stepping down outward. One-size zoning misses that.');
CALL newPutForth(1263, 1, 'Rural students often face systemic disadvantages in college admissions — fewer AP courses, less college counseling, lower test prep resources. That\'s a structural gap, not a talent gap.');
CALL newPutForth(1266, 1, 'Representation initiatives should target structurally disadvantaged groups — defined by evidence of actual disadvantage. That\'s focused and defensible, unlike broad categorical preferences that may not track real need.');
CALL newPutForth(1267, 1, 'Meritocracy in its pure form doesn\'t exist — connections, wealth, and privilege all shape outcomes. But pursuing it as an ideal — rewarding demonstrated performance — is still worth the effort. The goal is real even if perfect achievement isn\'t.');
CALL newPutForth(1268, 1, 'For comparable roles and skills, rural workers are typically paid less than urban counterparts. That wage gap reflects structural factors — labor market density, employer concentration, lack of alternatives — not productivity differences.');
CALL newPutForth(1352, 1, 'Parking minimums require developers to build more parking than the market demands, raising construction costs and consuming land. The market should determine how much parking gets built, not a zoning code written for a different era.');
CALL newPutForth(1353, 1, 'Parking is not a public entitlement. It\'s a use of space with a real cost. Free or subsidized parking doesn\'t mean parking has no cost — it means someone else is paying for it, and that subsidy shapes how cities develop.');
CALL newPutForth(1355, 1, 'Mass transit works when people ride it. Ridership should be encouraged through good service, reasonable fares, and urban design that makes transit the convenient choice — not through coercion or punitive car policy.');
CALL newPutForth(1545, 1, 'Liberal cities have the political alignment and local authority to deliver housing abundance. The fact that many haven\'t — that they\'ve prioritized homeowner preferences over housing production — is a genuine progressive failure.');
CALL newPutForth(1546, 1, 'The housing crisis is primarily a supply problem. Demand is growing. The policy response is to build more housing — not restrict it, regulate it into unaffordability, or wait for someone else to solve it.');
CALL newPutForth(1547, 1, 'When someone shows up to a zoning hearing to oppose a new building because it would change neighborhood character, they usually mean they don\'t want more people living near them. That\'s not a legitimate planning objection.');
CALL newPutForth(1548, 1, 'Upzoning is a choice. It doesn\'t require new state legislation in most cases — just the willingness to change the zoning map. Every city claiming it can\'t solve its housing problem while maintaining exclusionary zoning is deceiving itself.');
CALL newPutForth(1549, 1, 'Building codes that require expensive materials or elaborate systems for small buildings add cost without proportional safety benefit. Some of these requirements exist to protect incumbent housing values, not actual safety.');
CALL newPutForth(1550, 1, 'Cities that want to be progressive should demonstrate it by building housing. Affordable housing isn\'t just a federal issue. Local zoning decisions shape who can afford to live there.');
CALL newPutForth(1551, 1, 'Let developers build what the market will absorb. Restricting development to manage affordability paradoxically makes affordability worse. More supply, even market-rate supply, moderates prices over time.');
CALL newPutForth(1552, 1, 'Communities have legitimate interests in how they develop — not unlimited veto power over every change, but a real voice in shaping character and pace. That\'s a fair balance between stability and growth.');
CALL newPutForth(1554, 1, 'Modern building codes — energy efficiency, seismic standards, accessibility — are improvements, but they add cost. Some of that cost is worth it. Some has become excessive and contributes directly to housing unaffordability.');
CALL newPutForth(1555, 1, 'Manufactured homes are stigmatized but functional. They cost less to build and can be deployed faster than site-built homes. If we\'re serious about the housing crisis, dismissing an entire housing category is a mistake.');
CALL newPutForth(1557, 1, 'Low-density sprawl consumes enormous land per household, requires car ownership for basic mobility, and is expensive to serve with infrastructure. Where we can pull growth back toward existing urban areas, we should.');
CALL newPutForth(1558, 1, 'If you want to protect forests and open land, live in a city. Urban density is the most efficient way to house people with a small geographic footprint. Every city resident is a person not clearing trees for a cul-de-sac.');
CALL newPutForth(1559, 1, 'Population density in cities reduces per-capita land consumption dramatically. A city of a million people occupies far less area than the same population dispersed in suburbs or exurbs. Concentrated settlement is our best conservation strategy.');
CALL newPutForth(1560, 1, 'A homeowner serving on city council making decisions about housing density has a direct financial stake in those decisions. That\'s a conflict of interest, and it produces policies that harm renters and future residents.');
CALL newPutForth(1561, 1, 'You won\'t fix the housing crisis if the decision-makers hold most of their net worth in home equity. Their personal financial interest is in policies that maintain scarcity. Recognize that conflict before expecting reform from within.');
CALL newPutForth(1570, 1, 'Induced demand is real and well-documented. Build a new freeway lane and traffic fills it. Build a new parking garage and cars materialize. Adding parking capacity doesn\'t solve parking problems — it encourages more driving.');
CALL newPutForth(1571, 1, 'Parking minimums don\'t solve on-street parking shortages. They produce off-street parking that eats building footprints, raises rents, and increases the number of cars in the area — which worsens on-street competition.');
CALL newPutForth(1573, 1, 'Requiring parking minimums in new development subsidizes car ownership in land costs. People who don\'t own cars pay the same rent for a building that required expensive parking infrastructure they\'ll never use.');
CALL newPutForth(1574, 1, 'Walkable commercial districts perform better than car-oriented ones. Pedestrians make more spontaneous stops, support more diverse local businesses, and generate more street life. Parking lots are the enemy of retail vitality.');
CALL newPutForth(1575, 1, 'In most American cities, roads, parking lots, and garages consume 30 to 50 percent of urban land. That\'s land that could house people, host businesses, or create public space. Car infrastructure is our most expensive land use.');
CALL newPutForth(1576, 1, 'Every acre of surface parking or highway right-of-way is an acre that could be buildings, parks, or anything else. The car\'s claim on urban land comes at a real cost to everything else that land could otherwise support.');




